rresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$”; “$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460558 :answer-field/key "I" "$v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186460559 :answer-field/key "II" "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186460560 :answer-field/key "III" "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186152384 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152384 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+5I:$\n\n$$\nA+5I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+5I:$\n\n$$\nA+5I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460561 :answer-field/key "I" "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186460562 :answer-field/key "II" "$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186460563 :answer-field/key "III" "$v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186152410 :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4b568fe2226154be401a2332c718f143954d98fdbd8a0c679fdb99fcdd47d14.png)\n\nSarah interviewed some parents, asking them how much time their children spend playing outdoors per day. The information she received is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n- There is only one child aged $6$ among those surveyed.\n- None of the children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "![](images/e4/e4b568fe2226154be401a2332c718f143954d98fdbd8a0c679fdb99fcdd47d14.png)\n\nSarah interviewed some parents, asking them how much time their children spend playing outdoors per day. The information she received is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152410 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3.5,$ namely, $(5,3.5).$ This means that only one child spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n\n![](images/01/0103370189c7156f9677ca576e2480bc99314cd312702fca3ea3e50cb4f7287a.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,2)$ and $(6,2.5).$ This means that there were two children of age $6$ among those surveyed.\n\n![](images/0a/0a8ca9628b48e643e1aa9e9f0216148881db237c2d29054b29f4377ad182503d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, two children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors. Those children are represented by the two dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/4d/4d9709c131045f2e440204f79ad3fd8ff22707312b0e99138da558393e8b7f6d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement “There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors” is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3.5,$ namely, $(5,3.5).$ This means that only one child spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n\n![](images/01/0103370189c7156f9677ca576e2480bc99314cd312702fca3ea3e50cb4f7287a.png)\n\n- Statement “There is only one child aged $6$ among those surveyed” is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,2)$ and $(6,2.5).$ This means that there were two children of age $6$ among those surveyed.\n\n![](images/0a/0a8ca9628b48e643e1aa9e9f0216148881db237c2d29054b29f4377ad182503d.png)\n\n- Statement “None of the children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors” is false. From the scatter plot, two children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors. Those children are represented by the two dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/4d/4d9709c131045f2e440204f79ad3fd8ff22707312b0e99138da558393e8b7f6d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460631 :answer-field/key "I" "There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors."] [:db/cas 17592186460632 :answer-field/key "II" "There is only one child aged $6$ among those surveyed."] [:db/cas 17592186460633 :answer-field/key "III" "None of the children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors."] [:db/cas 17592186152419 :question/problem "![](images/b2/b25aa3b36011df9f9a97ca55fce75b2bf53b6b35282d8d5896e348464d4bdd30.png)\n\nMr. Clark recorded the height and shoe size of the players on the basketball team he coaches. The information he collected is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n- Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall.\n- There are exactly two players taller than $185\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/b2/b25aa3b36011df9f9a97ca55fce75b2bf53b6b35282d8d5896e348464d4bdd30.png)\n\nMr. Clark recorded the height and shoe size of the players on the basketball team he coaches. The information he collected is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152419 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose vertical coordinate equals $9.5,$ namely, $(180,9.5)$ and $(195,9.5).$ This means that two players have a shoe size of  $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2058bf02025ff359b509db01022c5d2e956d67243094a33692b90d6f1c2eac8.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $195,$ namely, $(195,\\,9.5).$ This means that only one of the players has a height of $195\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4364dc5de11eed12840bc644b1392b36160adfdf739b96f282c2367d999fa.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement III is false. According to the scatter diagram, three players have a height greater than  $185\\,\\text{cm},$ represented by the three dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf6d496c56c3cdc5a47e1c09e7640d7a3caa05446a75b705c042a56c116cb98.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement “Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}.$” is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose vertical coordinate equals $9.5,$ namely, $(180,9.5)$ and $(195,9.5).$ This means that two players have a shoe size of  $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2058bf02025ff359b509db01022c5d2e956d67243094a33692b90d6f1c2eac8.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement “Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall” is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $195,$ namely, $(195,\\,9.5).$ This means that only one of the players has a height of $195\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4364dc5de11eed12840bc644b1392b36160adfdf739b96f282c2367d999fa.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement “There are exactly two players taller than $185\\,\\text{cm}.$” is false. According to the scatter diagram, three players have a height greater than  $185\\,\\text{cm},$ represented by the three dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf6d496c56c3cdc5a47e1c09e7640d7a3caa05446a75b705c042a56c116cb98.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460634 :answer-field/key "I" "Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186460635 :answer-field/key "II" "Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall."] [:db/cas 17592186460636 :answer-field/key "III" "There are exactly two players taller than $185\\,\\text{cm}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186152435 :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x-1<x.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- **II.** The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- **III.** $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x-1<x.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152435 :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & <x \\\\ -1 & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the final condition is true for all $x\\in Z,$ we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & <x \\\\ -1 & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the final condition is true for all $x\\in Z,$ we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$” is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$” is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$ is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$”; $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186477562 :answer-field/key "I" "The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$"] [:db/cas 17592186477563 :answer-field/key "II" "The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$"] [:db/cas 17592186477564 :answer-field/key "III" "$∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186152449 :question/problem "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$\n- **II.** The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$\n- **III.** $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152449 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since $1\\le n$ for all natural numbers $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(m)$ is true for some $m\\in N$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true. However, \n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis false, since $2≰1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in N\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$ is true, since $1\\le n$ for all natural numbers $n.$\n\n- Statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$” is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(m)$ is true for some $m\\in N$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true. However, \n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis false, since $2≰1.$\n\n- Statement $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$ is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in N\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ But, according to statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$”, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186477565 :answer-field/key "I" "$∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186477566 :answer-field/key "II" "The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$"] [:db/cas 17592186477567 :answer-field/key "III" "$∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186152453 :question/problem "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n- **I.** $x=1$\n- **II.** $x<0$\n- **III.** $|x|<5$\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152453 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x=1)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-3,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x\\ne 1.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x<0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=1,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x≮0.$\n\n- Condition III is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ satisfies $|x|<5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition $x=1$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x=1)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-3,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x\\ne 1.$\n\n- Condition $x<0$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x<0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=1,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x≮0.$\n\n- Condition $|x|<5$ is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ satisfies $|x|<5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $|x|<5$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460681 :answer-field/key "I" "$x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460682 :answer-field/key "II" "$x<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186460683 :answer-field/key "III" "$|x|<5$"] [:db/cas 17592186152455 :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}+2n-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n- **I.** $n=1$\n- **II.** $n<0$\n- **III.** $|n|<5$\n\nSelect all that apply." "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}+2n-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152455 :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =0 \\\\ (n+3)(n-1) & =0 \\\\ n & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=1)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=1,$ then ${n}^{2}+2n-3=0.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n<0)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =(-1)^{2}+2(-1)-3 \\\\  & =1-2-3 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<5)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then $|n|<5,$ but ${n}^{2}+2n-3\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =0 \\\\ (n+3)(n-1) & =0 \\\\ n & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition $n=1$ is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=1)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=1,$ then ${n}^{2}+2n-3=0.$\n\n- Condition $n<0$ is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n<0)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =(-1)^{2}+2(-1)-3 \\\\  & =1-2-3 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition $|n|<5$ is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<5)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then $|n|<5,$ but ${n}^{2}+2n-3\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $n=1$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460684 :answer-field/key "I" "$n=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186460685 :answer-field/key "II" "$n<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186460686 :answer-field/key "III" "$|n|<5$"] [:db/cas 17592186152500 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A$ containing all possible triangles in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x≅y\n$$\n\nRecall that $x≅y$ means that $x$ and $y$ are congruent.\n\nWhich of the following are true?\n- **I.** $R$ is reflexive\n- **II.** $R$ is symmetric\n- **III.** $R$ is transitive\n- **IV.** $R$ is an equivalence relation\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A$ containing all possible triangles in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x≅y\n$$\n\nRecall that $x≅y$ means that $x$ and $y$ are congruent.\n\nWhich of the following are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152500 :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $x\\in A,$ we have $x≅x.$ Every triangle is congruent with itself.\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. For all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x≅y,$ then $y≅x.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of rigid motions in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x≅y),$ then we can always find a sequence of rigid motions that maps $y$ to $x.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x≅y,$ and $y≅z,$ then $x≅z.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of rigid motions in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x≅y)$ and another sequence that maps $y$ to $z,$ then we can always find a sequence that maps $x$ to $z.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $x\\in A,$ we have $x≅x.$ Every triangle is congruent with itself.\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. For all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x≅y,$ then $y≅x.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of rigid motions in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x≅y),$ then we can always find a sequence of rigid motions that maps $y$ to $x.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x≅y,$ and $y≅z,$ then $x≅z.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of rigid motions in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x≅y)$ and another sequence that maps $y$ to $z,$ then we can always find a sequence that maps $x$ to $z.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$R$ is reflexive”; “$R$ is symmetric”; “$R$ is transitive”; “$R$ is an equivalence relation”."] [:db/cas 17592186460725 :answer-field/key "I" "$R$ is reflexive"] [:db/cas 17592186460726 :answer-field/key "II" "$R$ is symmetric"] [:db/cas 17592186460727 :answer-field/key "III" "$R$ is transitive"] [:db/cas 17592186460728 :answer-field/key "IV" "$R$ is an equivalence relation"] [:db/cas 17592186152502 :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={0,1,2}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(0,2),(2,0)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is reflexive\n- **II.** $R$ is symmetric\n- **III.** $R$ is transitive\n- **IV.** $R$ is an equivalence relation" "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={0,1,2}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(0,2),(2,0)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152502 :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because  $(0,0)\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* transitive because $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\in R,$ but $(0,0)\\notin R$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's neither reflexive nor transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because  $(0,0)\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* transitive because $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\in R,$ but $(0,0)\\notin R$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's neither reflexive nor transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$R$ is symmetric” only."] [:db/cas 17592186449282 :answer-field/key "I" "$R$ is reflexive"] [:db/cas 17592186449283 :answer-field/key "II" "$R$ is symmetric"] [:db/cas 17592186449284 :answer-field/key "III" "$R$ is transitive"] [:db/cas 17592186449285 :answer-field/key "IV" "$R$ is an equivalence relation"] [:db/cas 17592186152569 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **III.** The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152569 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460766 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186460767 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186460768 :answer-field/key "III" "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186152570 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **III.** The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152570 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460769 :answer-field/key "I" "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186460770 :answer-field/key "II" "${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186460771 :answer-field/key "III" "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186152586 :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6?$\n- **I.** ${R}_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$\n- **II.** ${R}^{5}$\n- **III.** ${\\text{M}}_{6}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 6$ matrices over $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $6?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152586 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{5}[t])=5+1=6.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{5})=5\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6}(R))={6}^{2}=36\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{5}[t])=5+1=6.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{5})=5\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6}(R))={6}^{2}=36\\ne 6.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is “${R}_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186477599 :answer-field/key "I" "${R}_{5}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5$"] [:db/cas 17592186477600 :answer-field/key "II" "${R}^{5}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477601 :answer-field/key "III" "${\\text{M}}_{6}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 6$ matrices over $R$"] [:db/cas 17592186152634 :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$\n- **II.** $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152634 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460932 :answer-field/key "I" "$y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460933 :answer-field/key "II" "$y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460934 :answer-field/key "III" "$y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186152731 :question/problem "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2},$ which of the following  statements are true? \n- **I.** The function is an injection\n- **II.** The function is a surjection\n- **III.** The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2},$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152731 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(2)=4=f(-2)$, but $2\\ne -2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is false. For instance, $f(2)=4=f(-2)$, but $2\\ne -2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186477676 :answer-field/key "I" "The function is an injection"] [:db/cas 17592186477677 :answer-field/key "II" "The function is a surjection"] [:db/cas 17592186477678 :answer-field/key "III" "The function is a bijection"] [:db/cas 17592186152732 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n- **I.** $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- **II.** $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152732 :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only real solutions of $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$, and neither belongs to $Q$.\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R$, with equality at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only real solutions of $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$, and neither belongs to $Q$.\n\n- “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R$, with equality at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186477679 :answer-field/key "I" "$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477680 :answer-field/key "II" "$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477681 :answer-field/key "III" "$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186152736 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n- **I.** $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- **II.** $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}-2$\n- **III.** $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=8x-3$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152736 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. For example, $1$ and $-1$ are distinct elements of $Q$, but $g(1)=-1=g(-1)$.\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- “$f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$” is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}-2$” is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. For example, $1$ and $-1$ are distinct elements of $Q$, but $g(1)=-1=g(-1)$.\n\n- “$h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=8x-3$” is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=8x-3$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186477682 :answer-field/key "I" "$f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$"] [:db/cas 17592186477683 :answer-field/key "II" "$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186477684 :answer-field/key "III" "$h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=8x-3$"] [:db/cas 17592186152737 :question/problem "![](images/06/06e3c48955dc8f907c87a93aeaa7c2a7240ea194fe1ca6a96b2a0bf566882264.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)=0.25{x}^{2}$, which of the following statements are true? \n- **I.** The function is an injection\n- **II.** The function is a surjection\n- **III.** The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/06/06e3c48955dc8f907c87a93aeaa7c2a7240ea194fe1ca6a96b2a0bf566882264.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)=0.25{x}^{2}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152737 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $∃\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](images/fc/fce55ada0375bff9bd3ea5852e80f707d70f145211b60e80b5a16b3049020000.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $∃\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](images/fc/fce55ada0375bff9bd3ea5852e80f707d70f145211b60e80b5a16b3049020000.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The function is a surjection” only."] [:db/cas 17592186477685 :answer-field/key "I" "The function is an injection"] [:db/cas 17592186477686 :answer-field/key "II" "The function is a surjection"] [:db/cas 17592186477687 :answer-field/key "III" "The function is a bijection"] [:db/cas 17592186152747 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- **II.** $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- **III.** $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152747 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$; $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186449415 :answer-field/key "I" "$11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$"] [:db/cas 17592186449416 :answer-field/key "II" "$27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$"] [:db/cas 17592186449417 :answer-field/key "III" "$30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152754 :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nI. $[-1]$\n\nII. $[-i]$\n\nIII. $[2i]$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152754 :question/worked-solution "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[-1]$; $[-i]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460965 :answer-field/key "I" "$[-1]$"] [:db/cas 17592186460966 :answer-field/key "II" "$[-i]$"] [:db/cas 17592186460967 :answer-field/key "III" "$[2i]$"] [:db/cas 17592186152758 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- **II.** $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- **III.** $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152758 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$ is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$ is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186449418 :answer-field/key "I" "$19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$"] [:db/cas 17592186449419 :answer-field/key "II" "$22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$"] [:db/cas 17592186449420 :answer-field/key "III" "$20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152767 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n- **I.** $f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- **II.** $g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- **III.** $h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152767 :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the codomain. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the codomain. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- “$g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- “$h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$” is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186477688 :answer-field/key "I" "$f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477689 :answer-field/key "II" "$g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477690 :answer-field/key "III" "$h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$"] [:db/cas 17592186152792 :question/problem "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}?$\n\nI. $-12$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $24$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152792 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number $-12$”; “The number $24$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186460971 :answer-field/key "I" "The number $-12$"] [:db/cas 17592186460972 :answer-field/key "II" "The number $3$"] [:db/cas 17592186460973 :answer-field/key "III" "The number $24$"] [:db/cas 17592186152809 :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152809 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460980 :answer-field/key "I" "$y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460981 :answer-field/key "II" "$y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$"] [:db/cas 17592186460982 :answer-field/key "III" "$y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$"] [:db/cas 17592186152811 :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$\n- **II.** $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$\n- **III.** $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152811 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460983 :answer-field/key "I" "$y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460984 :answer-field/key "II" "$y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460985 :answer-field/key "III" "$y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152819 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9df0c9dd8b76ac337b6110fc92019924ad2ee1c4cc0da8216c27709b641a8451.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "![](images/9d/9df0c9dd8b76ac337b6110fc92019924ad2ee1c4cc0da8216c27709b641a8451.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152819 :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(0)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(0)$ but $-2\\ne 0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(-2)\\ne f(0)$ is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(0)=2.$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(0)$ but $-2\\ne 0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ is **one-to-one**” is false. Indeed, since statement “$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$” is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186460986 :answer-field/key "I" "$f(-2)\\ne f(0)$"] [:db/cas 17592186460987 :answer-field/key "II" "$∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186460988 :answer-field/key "III" "$f$ is **one-to-one**"] [:db/cas 17592186152823 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- **II.** ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186152823 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/66/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The $xy$ term integrates to zero over both regions by symmetry. Over $D$, the integral of $y^2$ is $\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-x^2)^3/3\\,\\mathrm{d}x=32/105$. Over $R$, it is $4\\int_{-1}^{1}y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y=8/3$. These values are different, so function II does not satisfy the required equality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/66/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$ and ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$. The $xy$ term integrates to zero over both regions by symmetry. Over $D$, the integral of $y^2$ is $\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-x^2)^3/3\\,\\mathrm{d}x=32/105$. Over $R$, it is $4\\int_{-1}^{1}y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y=8/3$. These values are different, so function ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$ does not satisfy the required equality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$; ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186477697 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477698 :answer-field/key "II" "${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186477699 :answer-field/key "III" "${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186152824 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186152824 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/73/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/73/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$ and $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$; $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186460994 :answer-field/key "I" "$R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$"] [:db/cas 17592186460995 :answer-field/key "II" "$R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186460996 :answer-field/key "III" "$R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186152826 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d09a23a1ae9f737c7f19ba83d6d20c3fbcea3c7deb71fa307264c90afabc6580.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & 